1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,904
The school asks for a first-minute neonatal sequence and correct
ventilation and compression coordination. We will connect the actions
to the physiology rather than memorize disconnected numbers.

2
00:00:11,904 --> 00:00:23,400
An apneic or gasping newborn needs timely useful breaths after the
appropriate initial steps. Sixty seconds is an urgency objective, not
a rule to wait for the clock.

3
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:35,568
The supplied sources contain meaningful conflicts, including old
compression thresholds and ratios. Those will be labelled and
corrected, not blended into a new hybrid algorithm.

4
00:00:35,568 --> 00:00:47,952
This session concerns transition at birth. A collapse later in infancy
needs its applicable current context and pathway rather than a
protocol selected only from the word neonate.

5
00:00:47,952 --> 00:00:58,440
Before birth, oxygen and carbon dioxide exchange occur through the
placenta. The umbilical vein carries relatively oxygen-rich blood
toward the fetus.

6
00:00:58,440 --> 00:01:09,480
The two umbilical arteries return blood toward the placenta. Artery
and vein describe direction relative to the heart, not a universal
high- or low-oxygen rule.

7
00:01:09,480 --> 00:01:17,088
The fetal lungs are fluid-filled and pulmonary vascular resistance is
high. They are not yet the principal gas-exchange route.

8
00:01:17,088 --> 00:01:29,784
After birth, effective lung aeration and ventilation become essential.
That is why the birth-resuscitation sequence emphasizes breathing
before simply adding compressions or medicines.

9
00:01:29,784 --> 00:01:42,048
The ductus venosus allows a portion of umbilical blood to bypass the
liver. Do not describe the fetal liver as receiving no useful flow or
the ductus as a direct lung shortcut.

10
00:01:42,048 --> 00:01:50,952
The foramen ovale provides a route between the right and left atria.
The ductus arteriosus connects pulmonary artery and aorta.

11
00:01:50,952 --> 00:02:00,600
These latter routes help divert flow away from the high-resistance
fetal lung circuit. The direction and amount of flow depend on
pressures and resistance.

12
00:02:00,600 --> 00:02:12,024
Tracing the route is more useful than memorizing three names. Explain
which organ is partly bypassed and why the circulation must change
after birth.

13
00:02:12,024 --> 00:02:21,264
Effective lung aeration supports oxygen transfer and lowers pulmonary
vascular resistance, allowing more blood to reach the pulmonary
circulation.

14
00:02:21,264 --> 00:02:32,280
Removal of the low-resistance placental circulation changes systemic
resistance and venous filling. Together, these changes alter the
pressures across the fetal shunts.

15
00:02:32,280 --> 00:02:42,528
Functional closure and later anatomical closure are not identical. Do
not teach that every shunt becomes permanently closed at the instant
of birth.

16
00:02:42,528 --> 00:02:56,208
Poor ventilation, hypoxia, acidosis and cold can interfere with
successful transition. Supporting breathing and temperature therefore
treats linked physiology rather than isolated checkboxes.

17
00:02:56,208 --> 00:03:09,408
Ask about gestation, multiple fetuses, maternal illness, medicines and
delivery complications. Known risk guides preparation, but a baby
without risk factors may still need help.

18
00:03:09,408 --> 00:03:19,728
Name the newborn provider and ensure maternal observation continues. A
small crew should recognize competing tasks and request additional
resources early.

19
00:03:19,728 --> 00:03:29,304
Allocate timing, equipment and communication rather than have everyone
reach for the baby at once. Use clear requests and confirm what was
done.

20
00:03:29,304 --> 00:03:40,608
Agree cord management and the immediate support plan with other
clinicians present, including a midwife when involved. Individual
authority and competence still govern each action.

21
00:03:40,608 --> 00:03:50,856
Prepare a safe supported warm area and the appropriate thermal
supplies. Do not place an infant on an unstable cold surface while
searching for equipment.

22
00:03:50,856 --> 00:04:02,904
Verify mask sizes, valve function, pressure monitoring when available
and the gas-source arrangement of the actual device. Presence in a
sealed kit does not prove readiness.

23
00:04:02,904 --> 00:04:15,336
Have suction for obstruction, a clock, auscultation and appropriate
oxygenation monitoring. Do not make attachment of every monitor a
prerequisite for starting indicated breaths.

24
00:04:15,336 --> 00:04:27,504
Identify authorized backup devices and skilled assistance before
difficulty arises. A device that does not fit the newborn or is
outside the operator's role is not a usable backup.

25
00:04:27,504 --> 00:04:35,904
Record the complete-birth time. Quickly assess gestational context,
tone and whether breathing or crying is effective.

26
00:04:35,904 --> 00:04:46,560
A well baby can receive appropriate observed skin-to-skin care while
warmth and ongoing evaluation continue. Good first appearance is not
permission to stop watching.

27
00:04:46,560 --> 00:04:57,216
Poor tone, absent breathing or other concern leads to the appropriate
initial-support branch. Do not spend the first minute calculating a
complete Apgar before acting.

28
00:04:57,216 --> 00:05:08,400
Begin assessing heart rate and prepare ventilation as indicated. The
questions organize the response; they do not replace direct
examination or the full current algorithm.

29
00:05:08,400 --> 00:05:18,432
Evaporation removes heat as water leaves wet skin. For the appropriate
routine newborn, drying and replacing wet material reduce that loss.

30
00:05:18,432 --> 00:05:30,504
Convection involves cooler air, especially drafts, while conduction
involves direct contact with a cold surface. Warm the area and prepare
a suitable supported surface.

31
00:05:30,504 --> 00:05:41,664
Radiation transfers heat toward nearby cooler objects even without
contact. A baby can lose heat despite lying on a warm blanket if the
surroundings remain cold.

32
00:05:41,664 --> 00:05:55,032
Cold stress increases metabolic demand and can worsen oxygen and
glucose balance. The mechanism explains why thermal protection must
continue through transfer, not only immediately after birth.

33
00:05:55,032 --> 00:06:08,664
Current M zero nine uses an axillary range of thirty-six point five to
thirty-seven point five degrees Celsius. Keep this separate from the
older L zero two and textbook temperature wording.

34
00:06:08,664 --> 00:06:23,208
A routine term baby needs appropriate drying, removal of wet material,
covering and observation. Very preterm care can require wrapping
without the same drying sequence; use the trained gestation-specific
plan.

35
00:06:23,208 --> 00:06:34,728
If a thermal bag or wrap is used, keep the face and airway accessible
and do not secure it around the neck. Monitor temperature and the
ongoing clinical condition.

36
00:06:34,728 --> 00:06:47,736
Avoid direct unregulated heat and hot-water bottles. Do not initiate
therapeutic cooling from this teaching; specialized cooling decisions
do not replace prevention of accidental hypothermia.

37
00:06:47,736 --> 00:06:58,272
Perform appropriate warmth and positioning promptly. Avoid a flexed
chin or excessive neck extension and check that support actually helps
airway patency.

38
00:06:58,272 --> 00:07:08,016
Use gentle stimulation such as appropriate drying or rubbing the back.
Do not slap, shake or repeatedly apply harsh sole stimulation.

39
00:07:08,016 --> 00:07:15,984
Clear visible obstruction with the appropriate method when needed, not
as an obligatory routine before every first breath.

40
00:07:15,984 --> 00:07:26,184
Then act on breathing and heart rate without delay. Continuing
stimulation for another long cycle is not the answer to persistent
apnea or gasping.

41
00:07:26,184 --> 00:07:34,296
Initial warmth and evaluation can occur during deferred cord clamping.
The team should agree the plan before birth when possible.

42
00:07:34,296 --> 00:07:44,040
The current pages use different timing language, as explained in the
birth session. Keep vigorous-infant care and a newborn needing
immediate support distinct.

43
00:07:44,040 --> 00:07:54,480
Do not let a timer prevent effective breaths. If required care cannot
be provided with the cord intact, coordinate the appropriate
operational action rather than delay.

44
00:07:54,480 --> 00:08:05,136
Do not introduce cord milking, a compulsory below-placenta position or
a claimed half-blood-loss rule from unqualified old text. Use the
actual trained plan.

45
00:08:05,136 --> 00:08:16,800
Current professional guidance does not recommend routine suction
merely because fluid is clear or meconium-stained. A nonvigorous baby
still needs the breathing problem addressed promptly.

46
00:08:16,800 --> 00:08:29,064
If the airway appears obstructed, use appropriate authorized clearing
and reassess. Obstruction may involve secretions or other material,
but not every poor breath is a suction problem.

47
00:08:29,064 --> 00:08:41,448
The local M zero nine body and older Nancy text include broader or
contradictory meconium wording. Do not use that as permission for
prolonged routine suction before useful ventilation.

48
00:08:41,448 --> 00:08:52,464
Use the current operational instruction and appropriately trained help
if uncertainty persists. Airway clearing must improve ventilation
rather than become the task that prevents it.

49
00:08:52,464 --> 00:09:01,776
Assess heart rate promptly and report it to the team. Initial
precordial auscultation is useful while appropriate monitoring is
arranged.

50
00:09:01,776 --> 00:09:14,016
ECG can provide a rapid rate, while pulse oximetry also contributes
oxygenation information. A poor or delayed probe signal should not
stop an urgent ventilation decision.

51
00:09:14,016 --> 00:09:24,168
An electrical rate is not itself proof of mechanical output or
adequate oxygenation. Combine it with actual clinical response and
relevant findings.

52
00:09:24,168 --> 00:09:34,896
Do not spend a full minute searching for a perfect count while an
apneic newborn remains unsupported. Use the best reliable timely
method and reassess.

53
00:09:34,896 --> 00:09:45,216
After appropriate initial steps, apnea, gasping or a persistent heart
rate below one hundred requires assisted ventilation through the
current newborn pathway.

54
00:09:45,216 --> 00:09:53,424
Do not wait for a twenty-second pause to satisfy the old apnea
definition. Inadequate breathing is an action finding now.

55
00:09:53,424 --> 00:10:04,632
The aim is prompt effective ventilation, not merely starting to
squeeze a bag before a timer expires. Ensure useful air entry and
reassess the response.

56
00:10:04,632 --> 00:10:13,920
Apgar scoring and every monitor attachment are secondary to indicated
support. The score can be documented alongside ongoing care.

57
00:10:13,920 --> 00:10:24,480
The school outline and supplied textbook teach forty to sixty breaths
per minute for newborn ventilation. The inspected current BCEHS
algorithm prints thirty to sixty.

58
00:10:24,480 --> 00:10:36,312
Keep the school rehearsal requirement visible and verify the assessed
version with the instructor while recognizing the current operational
range. Do not silently relabel one as the other.

59
00:10:36,312 --> 00:10:46,440
Use a controlled rhythm with effective breaths and appropriate
release. A rate within a range does not prove useful ventilation if
there is leak or obstruction.

60
00:10:46,440 --> 00:10:56,952
Compression coordination and older-child rescue breathing are
different situations. Announce the algorithm and branch so the team
does not mix its numbers.

61
00:10:56,952 --> 00:11:07,656
Choose the appropriate mask size and fit rather than cover the eyes or
rely on a poorly fitting edge. Use the actual device instructions and
trained technique.

62
00:11:07,656 --> 00:11:16,392
Maintain an open supported airway. Too much neck extension, flexion or
pressure into soft tissues can worsen the problem.

63
00:11:16,392 --> 00:11:25,008
Use a trained two-provider approach when it improves positioning and
seal, allocating bag operation separately from airway support.

64
00:11:25,008 --> 00:11:33,984
Observe the actual breath effect. A correctly held-looking mask with
no useful inflation is not a completed ventilation task.

65
00:11:33,984 --> 00:11:44,808
A self-inflating bag and a flow-inflating system do not work in the
same way. Know which device is actually in your hands and what it
needs to deliver a breath.

66
00:11:44,808 --> 00:11:57,000
A self-inflating bag can provide air ventilation without an oxygen
source, while other systems need the appropriate gas supply. That does
not mean every bag gives effective free-flow oxygen.

67
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:10,296
Check oxygen concentration arrangements, valves and pressure-
monitoring controls according to the actual device. Do not assume a
reservoir or connected tubing establishes a known delivered
concentration.

68
00:12:10,296 --> 00:12:21,264
Practise setup and failure recognition on the available equipment.
This explanation does not certify an uninspected model or justify
overriding its safety features.

69
00:12:21,264 --> 00:12:32,112
Look for useful chest movement and a rising heart rate after assisted
breaths. The physiological response is more important than the number
of times a bag was squeezed.

70
00:12:32,112 --> 00:12:44,400
If heart rate does not rise, reassess ventilation quality and the
clinical situation. Leak, airway position, obstruction or equipment
failure can prevent lung inflation.

71
00:12:44,400 --> 00:12:54,216
Thirty seconds of ineffective bagging does not equal thirty seconds of
ventilation that inflated the lungs. Do not progress simply because
time passed.

72
00:12:54,216 --> 00:13:03,792
Reassess after each correction while requesting help as needed.
Continued poor response can also reflect other pathology that needs
advanced evaluation.

73
00:13:03,792 --> 00:13:14,040
The actual linked BCEHS corrective table begins with mask adjustment
and repositioning. These address common causes of leak and ineffective
airway alignment.

74
00:13:14,040 --> 00:13:24,504
A trained two-hand seal may help while another provider operates the
bag. Keep the airway neutral or slightly extended rather than force it
into an extreme position.

75
00:13:24,504 --> 00:13:34,176
After those corrections, provide breaths and reassess movement and
heart-rate response. The table explicitly includes a reassessment
point.

76
00:13:34,176 --> 00:13:44,232
Do not perform every later step automatically when the first
correction works. The purpose is effective ventilation, not completion
of a mnemonic.

77
00:13:44,232 --> 00:13:55,344
If obstruction is suspected, use appropriate clearing of mouth and
nose within the actual procedure. Suction is not required solely
because the letter S appears in the mnemonic.

78
00:13:55,344 --> 00:14:04,800
Opening the mouth and supporting the jaw can improve the airway
through the trained method. Avoid compressing soft tissues or causing
unnecessary trauma.

79
00:14:04,800 --> 00:14:14,856
Try ventilation again and check the effect before further escalation.
Communicate what changed and whether useful inflation or rate response
occurred.

80
00:14:14,856 --> 00:14:24,984
Unnecessary repeated suction can delay ventilation and worsen
bradycardia or injury. Stay focused on the actual barrier and the
newborn's response.

81
00:14:24,984 --> 00:14:38,064
The local MR.SOPA pressure row prints increments of five to ten
centimeters of water and a maximum of forty. Identify that as the
table's corrective reference, not a routine starting prescription.

82
00:14:38,064 --> 00:14:49,824
Actual pressure use requires the correct device, monitoring,
gestational context and trained pathway. The aim is effective lung
inflation with the least harmful suitable pressure.

83
00:14:49,824 --> 00:14:59,976
Do not cover a pop-off valve or repeatedly increase force merely
because the old textbook describes it. Excess volume and pressure can
injure the lung.

84
00:14:59,976 --> 00:15:10,512
Check for an unresolved leak, obstruction or system problem and seek
appropriate help. Pressure escalation cannot compensate for every
failure mode.

85
00:15:10,512 --> 00:15:22,632
When useful ventilation cannot be established, an appropriate
alternative airway may be needed. Arrange the right trained resource
rather than persist indefinitely with ineffective breaths.

86
00:15:22,632 --> 00:15:34,128
Current M zero nine lists supraglottic airway and PEEP in the PCP
section, but actual device fit, eligibility, training and procedure
requirements still apply.

87
00:15:34,128 --> 00:15:44,664
Intubation and specialized neonatal support remain separate level-
dependent actions. A diagram that says intubate does not authorize
every person who reads it.

88
00:15:44,664 --> 00:15:56,040
After any device change, verify useful ventilation and reassess. A
placed airway is not automatically a functioning airway, especially
after movement.

89
00:15:56,040 --> 00:16:09,552
A term newborn starting assisted ventilation is not automatically
given one hundred percent oxygen. The current local initial-
resuscitation instruction uses room air with saturation-guided
adjustment.

90
00:16:09,552 --> 00:16:20,184
Use the appropriate right-hand or wrist preductal probe and verify a
reliable signal. Saturation and heart-rate measurements answer
different questions.

91
00:16:20,184 --> 00:16:32,544
Prematurity can change oxygen requirements; do not infer one
concentration for every gestation. Follow the actual trained local
plan and seek suitable neonatal support.

92
00:16:32,544 --> 00:16:45,288
The compression branch uses one hundred percent oxygen in the current
algorithm, followed by appropriate titration when compressions are no
longer needed. Do not transplant that phase into all routine birth
care.

93
00:16:45,288 --> 00:16:54,456
The inspected current algorithm gives sixty-five to seventy percent at
two minutes and seventy to seventy-five at three minutes after birth.

94
00:16:54,456 --> 00:17:03,576
At four minutes it lists seventy-five to eighty, at five minutes
eighty to eighty-five, and at ten minutes eighty-five to ninety-five
percent.

95
00:17:03,576 --> 00:17:14,616
These timed targets explain why a newly born infant does not instantly
need an adult ninety-four-percent target. Do not use them to excuse
apnea or failing ventilation.

96
00:17:14,616 --> 00:17:27,336
Use the actual algorithm and reliable monitoring, with clinical
response. Do not extrapolate an unprinted exact value between every
minute or treat a noisy number as adequate oxygenation.

97
00:17:27,336 --> 00:17:36,096
Current birth guidance adds compressions when heart rate remains below
sixty after thirty seconds of ventilation that effectively inflates
the lungs.

98
00:17:36,096 --> 00:17:49,152
Optimize ventilation, including an appropriate alternative airway when
feasible and authorized. Ineffective bagging time must not be mistaken
for the required useful ventilation interval.

99
00:17:49,152 --> 00:18:08,208
The June twenty twenty-six comparison uses a below-one-hundred
compression gate. The recovered March twenty twenty-five copy instead
prints below sixty at all three staged gates, which can delay
ventilation earlier. Neither dated loop replaces the current
ventilation and compression decisions.

100
00:18:08,208 --> 00:18:19,296
The original diagram emphasizes the separate decision gates. It is a
reasoning aid, not a complete device procedure or permission to bypass
the current full algorithm.

101
00:18:19,296 --> 00:18:28,224
Practise the two-thumb encircling technique on the actual training
manikin, supporting the torso with the fingers while thumbs compress
the sternum.

102
00:18:28,224 --> 00:18:37,728
Use the lower third of the sternum while avoiding the xiphoid. Do not
select a lower landmark solely from the nipple line or press into the
upper abdomen.

103
00:18:37,728 --> 00:18:48,984
Aim for about one third of the chest anterior-posterior diameter,
allow full recoil and avoid leaning. Exact practical positioning needs
instructor observation.

104
00:18:48,984 --> 00:18:59,184
The old two-finger illustration is not the preferred birth technique
taught here. Compression quality and synchronized effective
ventilation must be assessed together.

105
00:18:59,184 --> 00:19:09,168
The school and current newborn pathway use three compressions
synchronized to one ventilation. Practise a coordinated four-event
cycle with the team.

106
00:19:09,168 --> 00:19:20,112
The goal is ninety compressions and thirty breaths per minute: one
hundred twenty total events. That does not mean one hundred twenty
compressions plus extra breaths.

107
00:19:20,112 --> 00:19:33,888
The supplied Nancy paragraph mixes this newborn context with thirty-
to-two, fifteen-to-two and one hundred to one hundred twenty
compressions. Do not practise those mixed instructions as birth
resuscitation.

108
00:19:33,888 --> 00:19:45,960
Maintain useful breaths, recoil and communication, and minimize
unnecessary interruptions. A memorized count without depth and
ventilation effect is not adequate performance.

109
00:19:45,960 --> 00:19:56,904
After the coordinated support interval, reassess heart rate and
clinical response using the current pathway. Do not stop useful care
for an unnecessarily long count.

110
00:19:56,904 --> 00:20:08,112
A heart rate of eighty is above the compression threshold but still
below one hundred. Compressions may stop while effective ventilation
continues, depending on the actual findings.

111
00:20:08,112 --> 00:20:18,888
Even a rate above one hundred does not establish adequate spontaneous
breathing. Continue or reduce support according to breathing and the
full response, not rate alone.

112
00:20:18,888 --> 00:20:31,368
Persistent severe bradycardia needs verification of effective
ventilation and compressions and appropriate advanced escalation. Do
not repeatedly cycle through ineffective actions.

113
00:20:31,368 --> 00:20:43,632
If heart rate remains below sixty despite sixty seconds of coordinated
compressions and adequate ventilation, the current framework calls for
advanced escalation including medicine consideration.

114
00:20:43,632 --> 00:20:55,104
Current M zero nine places vascular access, intubation and epinephrine
beyond the PCP operational section, with specialized umbilical access
separately restricted.

115
00:20:55,104 --> 00:21:07,416
Do not give an algorithm-box dose or assume a term baby exceeds a
weight limit. Actual eligibility, reliable weight, device suitability
and the appropriate provider are required.

116
00:21:07,416 --> 00:21:19,896
Continue effective basic support while the team considers other causes
such as blood loss or pneumothorax. This session does not prescribe a
PCP neonatal infusion or invasive rescue procedure.

117
00:21:19,896 --> 00:21:33,072
After response, continue frequent appropriate observation of
breathing, rate, oxygenation and responsiveness. A single cry or
improved number does not establish sustained recovery.

118
00:21:33,072 --> 00:21:43,008
Maintain suitable thermal protection and check devices after movement.
Support that worked on the birth surface may become displaced during
transfer.

119
00:21:43,008 --> 00:21:54,000
Watch for recurrent apnea, worsening effort, poor perfusion or altered
tone. Reassess the whole situation rather than assume the original
problem cannot recur.

120
00:21:54,000 --> 00:22:04,320
Arrange the appropriate receiving center, resources and notification.
Do not use a reassuring Apgar or a complete cord cut as a discharge
decision.

121
00:22:04,320 --> 00:22:18,240
Consider neonatal glucose in the relevant illness, feeding,
temperature and gestational context through the applicable pathway.
Not every healthy term baby needs routine screening immediately after
birth.

122
00:22:18,240 --> 00:22:30,576
The school outline incorrectly generalizes a neonatal two-point-six
threshold to children older than one month. The pediatric lecture
distinguishes that from the current general below-four pathway.

123
00:22:30,576 --> 00:22:44,280
A neonatal D ten infusion described in a book or advanced protocol
does not give PCP independent neonatal IV authority. Check the actual
provider, age, route and consultation requirements.

124
00:22:44,280 --> 00:22:56,640
Feeding requires a safe clinical condition and effective airway
protection. Do not put milk or glucose into an inadequately responsive
or poorly breathing baby as a substitute for urgent support.

125
00:22:56,640 --> 00:23:08,016
Tell the family in clear short language that the baby needs help
breathing or circulation support and what the team is doing now. Avoid
a technical score as the whole explanation.

126
00:23:08,016 --> 00:23:16,872
Maintain privacy and appropriate family involvement while preserving
space for effective care. Assign a communicator when personnel allow.

127
00:23:16,872 --> 00:23:26,592
Do not promise a normal outcome or announce permanent disability from
a low early score. A population association is not an individual
prediction.

128
00:23:26,592 --> 00:23:37,224
Do not use a ten-minute old-text passage or a clock alone to terminate
care. Follow current guidance and consultation with clinical context
and family participation.

129
00:23:37,224 --> 00:23:49,296
In this original case, a newborn at an estimated thirty-nine weeks and
one day has poor tone, gasping and an auscultated heart rate of eighty
after appropriate initial steps.

130
00:23:49,296 --> 00:24:00,864
State the current problem: breathing is ineffective and heart rate is
below one hundred. Initiate useful assisted ventilation through the
current pathway without waiting for Apgar.

131
00:24:00,864 --> 00:24:10,560
Maintain the thermal and maternal-care roles while another provider
assists ventilation and monitoring. A birth emergency still involves
both patients.

132
00:24:10,560 --> 00:24:22,248
Do not begin compressions solely because eighty is below the old page-
forty-four one-hundred gate. Use the actual effective-ventilation and
persistent-sixty decision process.

133
00:24:22,248 --> 00:24:34,512
Assume early breaths produce little chest movement and an audible mask
leak, with the rate still around eighty. State the limitation rather
than call this successful ventilation.

134
00:24:34,512 --> 00:24:43,632
Correct mask fit and airway position using the trained approach, with
help for the seal where appropriate. Then give breaths and reassess.

135
00:24:43,632 --> 00:24:53,664
Do not advance solely because a timer says thirty seconds passed. No
useful inflation means the effective-ventilation interval has not been
demonstrated.

136
00:24:53,664 --> 00:25:05,232
Continue the correction pathway and call appropriate help if
ventilation remains ineffective. The case tests cause, intervention
and measured response, not mnemonic speed.

137
00:25:05,232 --> 00:25:15,024
Now assume an improved seal produces useful chest movement, heart rate
rises to one hundred thirty-two and spontaneous breathing becomes
effective.

138
00:25:15,024 --> 00:25:22,944
That response supports the importance of ventilation. It does not
justify adding compressions because the original rate was eighty.

139
00:25:22,944 --> 00:25:34,656
Reassess breathing, reliable oxygenation, tone and temperature and
adjust support through the current pathway. Do not stop needed support
solely because one number improved.

140
00:25:34,656 --> 00:25:45,144
Record times, corrections and responses and arrange indicated post-
resuscitation assessment. The newborn remains a separate patient
needing continued care.

141
00:25:45,144 --> 00:25:57,360
For an alternative original branch, assume thirty seconds of effective
ventilation has been demonstrated but heart rate remains fifty. That
meets the current compression decision context.

142
00:25:57,360 --> 00:26:09,552
Provide trained synchronized three-to-one support, compression-phase
oxygen and appropriate airway/advanced resource planning. Verify both
compression and breath quality.

143
00:26:09,552 --> 00:26:20,064
After the coordinated interval, assume rate rises to eighty. Stop
compressions as indicated by the current pathway while continuing the
ventilation still needed.

144
00:26:20,064 --> 00:26:31,680
If instead it remains below sixty after adequate coordinated support,
escalate with the appropriate advanced team. Do not infer a PCP drug
or IO permission from this case.

145
00:26:31,680 --> 00:26:42,000
Give estimated gestation, birth context and relevant maternal
concerns, with source reliability. Distinguish the baby's record from
the maternal record.

146
00:26:42,000 --> 00:26:52,920
State birth time, initial breathing and heart rate, when effective
ventilation was achieved and whether compressions were needed. Do not
report only bagging started.

147
00:26:52,920 --> 00:27:04,272
Report response, oxygen context, thermal findings, support and timed
Apgar observations. Note which readings were reliable and which
remained uncertain.

148
00:27:04,272 --> 00:27:13,800
Finish with current needs and the coordinated receiving plan. A clear
trajectory is more useful than a list of actions without their
effects.

149
00:27:13,800 --> 00:27:24,456
For the first-minute drill, identify the birth clock and demonstrate
the initial steps without delaying indicated ventilation. Explain how
you know a breath worked.

150
00:27:24,456 --> 00:27:35,496
For corrective ventilation, change one problem at a time where
feasible and reassess. Do not claim success because all MR.SOPA
letters were recited.

151
00:27:35,496 --> 00:27:47,880
For the compression branch, demonstrate the current threshold context,
technique, three-to-one rhythm and response-driven next step. State
why the old mixed ratios are not being used.

152
00:27:47,880 --> 00:28:00,000
Instructor observation on the actual training equipment is required
for practical signoff. This course prepares the reasoning and sequence
but does not certify hands-on resuscitation.

153
00:28:00,000 --> 00:28:11,064
You should explain why lung aeration changes circulation and why
warmth protects oxygen and glucose balance. Mechanism makes the
sequence easier to remember correctly.

154
00:28:11,064 --> 00:28:19,608
Provide timely useful breaths, test corrections and recognize when the
persistent-below-sixty branch requires synchronized support.

155
00:28:19,608 --> 00:28:28,704
Keep the school forty-to-sixty rate, current thirty-to-sixty rate,
three-to-one compression sequence and older-child rescue breathing
distinct.

156
00:28:28,704 --> 00:28:42,216
Then maintain observation, communicate honestly and arrange
appropriate care. The accompanying readings identify source conflicts
and unresolved school-page locators without pretending they are
complete.

157
00:28:42,216 --> 00:29:21,744
The literal M10 opening distinguishes surfactant-related RDS, retained
lung fluid in TTN, infection and air leak. These are possible
mechanisms, not a diagnosis from gestational age or a reason to wait
for a described72-hour course. Its later-neonatal saturation examples
and specialist oxygen/ventilator settings are not first-minute targets
or a PCP equipment recipe. Assess breathing effectiveness and
deterioration, support within the actual pathway and obtain
appropriate help; do not seek a printed high opening pressure with a
hand-held bag.

158
00:29:21,744 --> 00:30:00,720
Original contrast: a four-day-old has poor feeding, grunting and less
interaction than usual. The family recalls that low saturations can be
normal immediately after birth. That birth-minute table cannot
reassure this later presentation. Perform the primary assessment,
verify measurement quality without delaying indicated support, protect
temperature and communicate the age in days, baseline change,
breathing/perfusion findings and response. An apparently term birth or
a previous normal check does not exclude infection, congenital disease
or another current threat.

159
00:30:00,720 --> 00:30:43,704
The recovered March2025 page44 differs materially from the June2026
comparison: its three progression gates all say below60, while the
later copy says below100. Neither staged loop should replace the
current algorithm. The2025 first gate could withhold useful
ventilation from an apneic baby or a persistently slow rate of60-99;
the2026 later gate could add compressions too early at those rates.
Current birth guidance separates assisted ventilation for
apnea/gasping or persistent rate below100 from compressions for
persistent below60 after effective ventilation and correction.

160
00:30:43,704 --> 00:31:20,784
Original threshold check: after the appropriate initial actions, a
newly born infant is gasping and the measured heart rate remains92. Do
not wait for it to fall below60 before starting indicated useful
ventilation, and do not start compressions simply because it is
below100. Establish lung inflation and reassess the response using the
current birth pathway. Thirty seconds of ineffective bag squeezing is
not thirty seconds of effective ventilation. Keep the school reference
date visible during rehearsal and ask for the assessed revision rather
than inventing a hybrid number.
